[=#listagg_to_string_agg]
= listagg_to_string_agg =

The '''listagg_to_string_agg''' module rewrites Oracle/DB2-style //listagg()// aggregate calls as PostgreSQL //string_agg()// calls.  It's useful when running an application originally written for Oracle, DB2, or another database that supports //listagg()//, against PostgreSQL or another database that supports //string_agg()// but not //listagg()//.

A query like:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
select grp, listagg(name,',') within group (order by id asc)
from testtable group by grp
}}}
}}}

Would be translated to:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
select grp, string_agg(cast(name as text),',' order by id asc)
from testtable group by grp
}}}
}}}

An example configuration:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
@parts/sqlrelay-querytranslations-listagg-to-string-agg.conf@
}}}
}}}

The module has no module-specific attributes - only the standard '''module''', '''enabled''', and '''debug''' attributes apply.

Note that, in this example, the normalize translation is also configured.  Loading normalize first helps the module identify //listagg()// calls more reliably, as their syntactic form (the spacing around //within group//, the parenthesized //order by// clause, etc.) is sensitive to whitespace and case.

Note that in this example, the normalize query translation is loaded prior to the listagg_to_string_agg query translation.  Pattern matching is substantially more reliable and efficient if the query has been normalized first.  See the [normalize.html#normalize normalize] query translation for more information.

=== Considerations ===

The module performs the following transformations on each //listagg(EXPR, SEP) within group (order by SORT)// call:

* wraps the aggregated expression in '''cast(... as text)''', because //string_agg()// requires a text-typed first argument while //listagg()// accepts any type that can be coerced to a string
* moves the //order by// from the //within group// clause inside the //string_agg()// argument list, which is where PostgreSQL expects it

Calls inside string literals are left alone.  Identifiers like //mylistagg(...)// that merely end in //listagg// are also left alone.

The match is fairly strict.  A //listagg()// call is only rewritten if it is followed by '' within group ('' (a single space, then the keywords, then a space, then the open paren) and the contents of the //within group// clause begin with //order by // (lowercase, single space).  If you have queries with unusual spacing or case, run the [normalize.html#normalize normalize] translation first; otherwise the call will be passed through to the database untranslated and will fail there.

The module does not implement the //on overflow truncate// or //on overflow error// clauses, and does not implement //listagg()// used as a window function (//over (...)//).  Calls using either of these forms will not match and will be passed through untranslated.

